GRID-DIP METER WITH 6E5 "PEEPHOLE" (MAGIC-EYE) INDICATOR
Source: Radio-Electronics, March 1960 (classic ham-radio GDO)

TUBE TYPES
  V1  6C4     miniature RF triode (oscillator)
  V2  6E5     electron-ray / magic-eye tube (visual indicator)

POWER
  T   117 V AC  →  125 V @ 15 mA  +  6.3 V @ 600 mA heaters
  RECT  selenium 130 V / 20 mA
  C3   16 µF + 16 µF  150 V electrolytic
  R3   47 Ω
  R4   2.2 kΩ / 1 W

OSCILLATOR (tickler / Hartley-style plug-in coils)
                  L1-a (plate winding)
  +130 V ──┬── L1-a ── plate (pin 5) of 6C4
           │
           └── C1 (variable, matches coil set)
                  │
                  └── L1-b (grid / tickler winding)
                           │
                           C2 500 pF
                           │
                      grid (pin 6) of 6C4
                           │
                      R1 22 kΩ
                           │
                          GND
  cathode (pin 7) of 6C4 ── GND

INDICATOR
  6C4 grid ── R2 100 kΩ ── grid (pin 3) of 6E5
  6E5 target / plate (pin 4) ── +130 V via R5 1 MΩ
  6E5 cathode (pin 5) ── GND
  6E5 heater (pins 2+7) ── 6.3 V

FUNDAMENTALS
  • 6C4 runs as class-C RF oscillator. Grid leak produces negative DC bias
    proportional to oscillation amplitude.
  • When an external resonant circuit is loosely coupled to L1, it absorbs
    energy at resonance → oscillation amplitude drops → grid voltage of 6C4
    becomes less negative.
  • That change is applied to the 6E5 control grid, shrinking the dark
    “shadow angle” of the glowing green eye (the “peephole”).
  • Loose coupling is essential; too-tight coupling broadens the dip and
    ruins accuracy.
  • Plug-in coils set the frequency range; C1 is the main tuning control.

TRANSISTOR VARIANT (also shown in the original post)
  Colpitts / Hartley with BF495
  Lx + variable C form the tank
  1N34 germanium diode + 0-50 µA meter detect RF amplitude
  9 V battery, 47 kΩ pot for sensitivity
  Same principle: resonance of external circuit loads the oscillator
  and the meter reading dips.
